# Accept a transferred call
#
# A Flex agent hands a live call to the virtual agent, either by cold transferring it or
# by conferencing the assistant in. Nothing on the Flex side will ever accept a
# reservation offered to the virtual agent worker, so this system accepts it and dials
# the assistant onto the call the customer is already on.
#
# Code: controllers/event-taskrouter-post.go (handleVoiceReservationCreated)
#       models/virtual-agent-transfer.go
#       controllers/event-openai-post.go

title: |md
  # Accept a warm transferred call
  A Flex agent transfers a live call to the assistant, cold or by conferencing it in.
| {near: top-center}

shape: sequence_diagram

customer: Customer
agent: Flex agent
voice: Twilio Voice and Conference
taskrouter: Twilio TaskRouter
app: zenn-ai-assistant
openai: OpenAI Realtime API

# Precondition: customer and Flex agent are already talking on a conference.

agent -> taskrouter: Transfers to the virtual agent worker, cold or conference
taskrouter -> taskrouter: Creates a task carrying transferMeta and reserves the virtual agent worker
taskrouter -> app: POST /twilio/taskrouter/event (reservation.created)

decide: Is this ours to answer {
  app -> app: Worker attributes say isVirtualAgent and the channel is voice
  app -> app: transferMeta present, or no isVirtualAgentTask, so it is a hand-off

  # The assistant's own inbound reservation is answered by the queue assignment callback.
  # Answering that one here as well would put the assistant on the call twice.

  app -> app: transferMeta mode COLD becomes cold, WARM becomes conference
  app -> app: Conference sid from task attributes, else looked up by originalTaskSid
}

connect: The assistant is dialled onto the call {
  app -> taskrouter: Accepts the reservation
  app -> voice: Adds the assistant SIP address to the conference
  app -> voice: SIP uri carries X-Zenn-Virtual-Agent-Mode and X-Zenn-Task-Sid
  app -> voice: end_conference_on_exit is true only for a cold transfer
  app -> taskrouter: Records worker_call_sid and virtual_agent_mode on the task
}

session: Assistant session starts {
  voice -> openai: SIP INVITE carrying the X-Zenn headers
  openai -> app: POST /openai/events/realtime-call-incoming
  app -> openai: Accepts the call and opens the websocket
  app -> app: Reads the mode straight off the SIP header

  # The mode rides in on the invite rather than being read back from task attributes.
  # A task lookup would race the attribute write against the assistant's first words.
}

cold: Cold transfer - the Flex agent has left {
  app -> openai: response.create to greet
  openai -> customer: Greets the customer and takes over the call
  openai -> app: Later, end_call
  app -> taskrouter: Completes the task
  app -> voice: Ends the conference
  app -> openai: Hangs up the assistant leg
}

conference: Conference in - the Flex agent is still on the call {
  app -> openai: conversation.item.create, system message to stay silent until addressed

  # No greeting. The customer is mid-conversation with a human, and the assistant would
  # otherwise talk over them.

  agent -> openai: Asks the assistant a question
  openai -> agent: Answers briefly
  openai -> app: Later, end_call
  app -> taskrouter: Completes the task
  app -> openai: Hangs up ONLY its own leg
  agent -> customer: Agent and customer keep the call

  # Ending the conference here would hang up on the agent and their customer.
}
